WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.149
Vsauce!

00:00:01.149 --> 00:00:07.129
Kevin here -- when KGB Chairman Yuri Andropov
was conducting a job interview, the man said,

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:09.750
“Let me tell you about myself...”

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:16.270
Andropov stopped him and asked, “What makes
you think that you know more about you than

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:17.750
we do?”

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:22.970
That’s really creepy -- and also probably
true.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:28.540
Data collected via self-reporting compared
to outside observation tends not to be the

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.540
same.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:36.080
You’d think that the most reliable way to
get crime data is to just ask the people involved,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.050
both the victims and the criminals.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.659
Going straight to the source makes the most
sense, right?

00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:52.219
WRONG -- because not only can we not trust
you, you can’t trust you.

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:57.760
Analyzing crimes, criminals, and victims is
rife with problems -- it’s a running theme

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.969
in my videos.

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:04.430
The Worst Math Ever Used in Court shows how
probability can be twisted and misapplied

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:10.580
to be flat out wrong -- and the Sally Clark
video showed how that can ruin lives.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:16.060
If you think eyewitness testimony is bulletproof,
ask Jennifer Thompson and Ronald Cotton.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.530
And if reducing crimes to objective data seems
smart, check out the mad lib that is the US

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.960
Federal Sentencing Guidelines or the data-driven
monster called CompStat.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:35.119
If getting reliable information from the criminal
justice system is too complex, why not make

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:41.180
it as simple as possible by collecting data
directly from the people involved in the crimes?

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.080
LET’S FIND OUT.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:49.850
Even in the 1940s and 1950s, researchers knew
systems were biased and highly variable, so

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:55.080
they wanted to bypass the bias by gathering
data from primary sources.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.869
That way there’s nothing getting in the
way of the truth.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.030
Except us.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:07.560
Some of the problems with self-reporting are
obvious -- you need to ask people the right

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.979
questions.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.950
Bad questions, bad responses.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.650
And you need to ask the right people, too.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.819
Great questions to the wrong people just…
aren’t useful at all.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:21.819
I don’t know what it’s like to go into
space.

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:23.250
Ask an actual astronaut.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.250
They would know.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.400
Especially the ones that have been into space.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.440
But even a really straightforward and objective
question like measuring the average height

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.930
and weight of the population can be a total
mess.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.959
We’re just dealing with numbers, right?

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:39.970
This should be simple.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.909
Let’s ruin this in three ways.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:43.909
One.

00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:49.220
Doctors and nurses measuring during hospital
appointments -- this is professional data

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.730
collection, the OPPOSITE of self-reporting;
Two.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.860
Tracking the average sizes of clothes purchased
over time, this one’s part professional,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.379
part self-reporting.

00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:02.390
Three.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.880
Just asking people.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.450
Self-reporting.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:13.110
Hospital measurements are going to be accurate…
but the sample is not likely to be representative

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.990
of the broader population.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:21.420
By default, acutely sick and chronically ill
people are overrepresented.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.599
The result is a great measurement of an atypical
population.

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:28.560
So bad data.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:35.050
Trends and styles affect the size of clothing
people buy -- are the $186.9 million worth

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:40.630
of JNCO jeans sold in 1998 an accurate measure
of weight that year?

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:45.730
Well, puffy pants still have the same waist
and inseam, right?

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.730
Maybe!?

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:53.659
Take a look at this chart detailing how “Size
8” is actually 10 completely different measurements

00:03:53.659 --> 00:03:56.120
depending on the retailer.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.230
Vanity sizing ruins the data.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:05.310
And do larger people buy more or fewer clothes
than smaller people to skew the data set?

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.750
And which stores should we even track?

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:08.750
Brooks Brothers?

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:09.750
Tommy Bahama?

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.300
Ross Dress For Less?

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.450
Who knows?!

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.280
Bad data.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:21.780
Okay, so let's just ask people -- a 2009 study
showed that men over-report their height by

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.530
about half an inch, and women under-report
their weight by an average of 3 lbs.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:33.650
It doesn’t mean anyone is lying -- they
probably really believe they’re giving accurate

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.740
information.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:41.440
The problem is that it’s wrong so… again
bad data.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.660
Is it hopeless?

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.340
YEAH, yeah, it’s probably hopeless.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:54.400
In 1964, a photographer asked if she could
shoot Andy Warhol’s Marilyn Monroe paintings.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.590
He thought she meant photos but she pulled
out a pistol and fired a shot through Marilyn's

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:00.590
forehead.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:07.350
It took 4 minutes for "Shot Sage Blue Marilyn"
to sell for $195 million -- which happened

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:12.540
as $9 trillion was wiped from the stock market
and inflation ran rampant.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:17.949
Morgan Stanley reports that the average fine
art piece is selling for 26% more at auction

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:23.360
compared to last year -- because art has some
unique qualities as an investment.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.860
You can buy shares in works from great artists
-- from Banksy to Picasso -- with Masterworks.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:34.229
They have over half a million members because
they've gotten results, like a sale in October

00:05:34.229 --> 00:05:38.380
that netted a 21.5% return for its investors.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.780
As a result, demand for Masterworks is so
high that there’s a wait list.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.290
But you can skip it by clicking the link in
the description for priority access -- and

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.690
do every bit of research you can.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.680
All investments carry risks, so be smart about
it.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.590
Go to Masterworks.art/Vsauce2 -- and thanks
to Masterworks for sponsoring this video.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.900
James Short and Ivan Nye sensed they were
getting misleading and incomplete crime data

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.130
in the 1950’s, and… they were.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:10.761
They recognized that criminals can manipulate
to sway the tone of interviews, so they threw

00:06:10.761 --> 00:06:16.650
in questions to expose over- or under-reporting
of delinquency.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.380
Like questions about whether criminals had
ever disobeyed their parents or ever lied

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:27.400
-- unrealistic answers like claiming you’ve
never once lied to your parents presented

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:34.660
an image of manipulative “over-conformity,”
so those respondents were excluded.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:40.819
So if you know data is routinely unreliable,
at least you can filter out the very worst

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:41.819
of it.

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:47.280
The thing is, most people do try hard to be
accurate -- but their own worldview and the

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.610
limits of language can dramatically skew the
results.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:56.030
Let’s use some of Short and Nye’s real
self-reporting questions to generate some

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:57.069
BAD DATA.

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:00.530
“Have you ever had a fist fight with one
person?”

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:05.950
-- Well, what does that mean exactly -- does
horsing around with a sibling that gets out

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:13.290
of hand constitute a fist-fight, are we talking
competitive combat in a kumite, or instigating

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:17.040
or defending yourself in a violent altercation?

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.690
Those are all pretty different fist fights.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:24.670
“Have you ever drank wine, beer, or liquor
in your own home?”

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:29.569
-- Does that mean stealing your dad’s bottle
of Pappy Van Winkle and guzzling it in your

00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:35.740
bedroom, or your mom giving you one single
sip of giggle juice on New Year’s Eve?

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:37.129
I don’t know.

00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:40.720
“Have you ever driven too fast or recklessly?”

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.740
To me, 15MPH over the speed limit is too fast.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.970
To my grandma, 2 MPH over the limit is a death
race.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:56.680
Individual judgment matters, and we can’t
guarantee that over- and under-interpretations

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.530
will cancel each other out to give us an accurate
picture.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:05.650
Probably the most obvious problem with self-reporting
is that…

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.889
people lie.

00:08:06.889 --> 00:08:11.780
Especially as the behavior to which they’d
be admitting -- even anonymously -- becomes

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.960
more serious.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.160
You’re more likely to admit to swiping a
sip of wine than overtaking an international

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.300
drug trafficking syndicate.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:26.440
But occasionally self-reporting gives us a
nugget of extremely valuable truth.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:32.789
In 1978, the RAND Corporation conducted surveys
of inmates in California penitentiaries to

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:38.000
assess their levels of criminal behavior prior
to their incarceration.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.820
Inmates were asked how many times per year
they committed each of the following crimes,

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.980
and they got a median value:

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:51.970
Burglary, Robbery, Assault, Theft, Forgery
and Credit Card fraud,Fraud, and

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.170
All crimes except drug dealing.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:59.740
We’ll say that someone at the 50th percentile
of crime frequency for each of these offenses

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:05.750
represents the ‘average’ criminal -- but
what about the criminals at the 90th percentile,

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:07.980
the very worst ones?

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.610
Burglary, 232
Robbery, 87

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:15.239
Assault, 13
Theft, 425

00:09:15.239 --> 00:09:20.430
Forgery and Credit Card fraud, 206
Fraud, 258

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:25.310
All crimes except drug dealing, 605

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.010
605.

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:32.830
8 thefts a year is fewer than 1 a month.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:35.880
425 is more than 1 a DAY.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:42.190
The differences here range from about 5x more
crime, like assault, to FIFTY times more on

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:49.391
thefts and fraud -- even if the median inmate
underreported his crimes and the top 10% worst

00:09:49.391 --> 00:09:56.490
criminals over-reported because they were
bragging, the data reveals a substantial gap

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:58.140
we didn’t know existed.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:04.470
We wouldn’t need the exact numbers to understand
that the criminal class is highly varied,

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:11.300
and that most criminals haven’t dedicated
their entire life to crime -- but a portion…

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.890
absolutely, quantifiably have.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:19.850
The good news is that informs our approach
to both punishment and rehabilitation.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:23.690
The bad news is… it doesn’t tell us the
truth.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:29.420
We don’t know exactly what the inmates considered
to be crimes, whether they were honest, or

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:32.180
whether they even remembered correctly.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:37.080
And it doesn’t answer the question about
how we can make sure criminals don’t re-offend,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:43.240
or whether there are people we truly will
never be able to help.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.790
But it does tell us something -- and it’s
up to us to use that information in conjunction

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:54.390
with everything else that we’ve measured
and collected.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:11:02.160
Sometimes that is helpful; sometimes we have
to admit it’s not.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:07.910
The strange thing is, we know we can’t trust
what people say...

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:15.110
But if we never ask, we’ll never know.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:27.019
And as always, thanks for watching.

